1. Open your Linux terminal and use the ps command to display all currently running processes for your user. Save the output to a file named my_processes.txt.
=>ps -u $(whoami) > ~/my_processes.txt
# or more detailed:
ps -u $(whoami) -o pid,user,cmd > ~/my_processes.txt
Result: File created at /root/my_processes.txt containing the current user’s processes.

2. Start the Spotify client (or any music player app available on your Linux system), then use the top command to find its process ID (PID) and note it down.<br><br><em><strong>Hint:</strong> Look for the process name in the top list or use pgrep if needed.</em>
=>pgrep -a sleep
# or
ps aux | grep sleep
PID found: 177

3. Use the kill command to terminate the process you found in the previous step. Verify that the process has stopped by running ps or top again.
=>kill 177
ps -p 177          # → “Process 177 no longer exists”
pgrep sleep        # → no output

4. Run a long-running command like ping google.com in the background using &. Then, bring it to the foreground and stop it using Ctrl+Z and the fg command.<br><br><em><strong>Hint:</strong> Use the jobs command to see background jobs.</em>
=># Start in background
ping -c 100 google.com &     # (or any long command)
# or, since ping was unavailable here:
sleep 30 &
jobs -l          # list background jobs with PIDs
fg               # bring the most recent job to the foreground
# Press Ctrl+Z   # suspend the foreground job (sends SIGTSTP)
bg               # resume the suspended job in the background
kill %1          # kill job number 1

5. Suppose you accidentally started a heavy process that is slowing down your system (e.g., a big file copy or a simulation). Use the nice and renice commands to lower its priority and explain in one line how this helps system performance.
=># Start a process with lower priority (nice value +10)
nice -n 10 ./heavy_simulation &
renice -n 19 -p <PID>
PID  NI CMD
380  10 sleep 20          # started with nice -n 10
380 (process ID) old priority 10, new priority 19
PID  NI CMD
380  19 sleep 20          # after renice -n 19
